{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChGsKPDveqfgWuUX2f1W9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V1Xg3Avc1sxdkMB1Fb1sWs","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md | cut -c1-300","description":"Read review doctrine severity section"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1629,"cache_read_input_tokens":125783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1629},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"b690d67d-e7d8-485e-827b-13666bdae208","timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:33:09.828Z","request_id":"req_011ChGsKP41FeSeHVqH2jzBT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01V1Xg3Avc1sxdkMB1Fb1sWs":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md | cut -c1-300","description":"Read review doctrine severity section"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1Xg3Avc1sxdkMB1Fb1sWs","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntitle: Review Core Doctrine\ndescription: Shared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"findings first severity ordering\"\n  - \"review finding schema\"\n  - \"severity contract for merge decisions\"\n  - \"evidence file line citation\"\n  - \"baseline surface precedence\"\n  - \"baseline check families\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.5.10.0\n---\n\n# Review Core Doctrine\n\nShared doctrine consumed by both `@review` and `@deep-review`.\n\n**Ownership.** This file owns the severity ids and their meanings (section 2), the evidence rules (section 3) and the finding schema (section 7). The deep-review contract, [`review-mode-contract.yaml`](../../../system-deep-loop/deep-review/assets/review-mode-contract.yaml), is an external consumer: \n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nShared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- A `@review` or `@deep-review` pass that needs the severity contract, the evidence rules or the finding schema.\n- A review that must state its `Rule sources:` line, or apply a repository or surface override to a default-lens rule.\n\nEvery rule in sections 2 to 7 is review floor, the part no repository or surface can change. Section 8 names the floor ids, the default lens that a repository may drop or replace, and how the review reads and reports those overrides.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SEVERITY DEFINITIONS\n\n| Level | Meaning | Handling |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P0 | Blocker: exploitable security issue, auth bypass, destructive data loss | Block merge |\n| P1 | Required: correctness bug, spec mismatch, must-fix gate issue | Fix before merge |\n| P2 | Suggestion: non-blocking improvement, documentation polish, style or maintainability follow-up | Optional or schedule follow-up |\n\n### Reach Calibration\n\nA `P0` or `P1` finding names its reach: an input or call path that the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input. The stated contract is what the code promises through its signature, types, doc comment, name and the checks it already makes.\n\n- A failure that needs input outside the stated contract, such as `NaN` where the contract says a number, a wrong type, or an inverted range the code already rejects, is `P2` hardening.\n- The exception is input the code claims to validate. When a guard, a thrown error or a doc line promises to reject an input class and the promise fails for an input in that class, the gap is a correctness finding and keeps `P1` or higher. A guard claims only the class it checks: an ordering check s\n- At a trust boundary (user input, network, files, environment, another service), every value the outside can send is in reach. The stated contract of internal code never narrows a security finding on data that crosses that boundary.\n- A finding that cannot name its reach is `P2`, however severe the failure would be.\n\nEscalation rule: if confidence is low but impact is high, classify toward the higher severity and state the uncertainty explicitly. The confidence is confidence that the reach exists. The rule raises a finding whose reach is named but not proven, such as a caller that probably sends the input. It ne\n\n### Numeric Severity Calibration\n\nNumeric scores are advisory context, not the gate. A reviewer may add an optional `riskScore` to a finding and adjust it by `+/-2` for local context such as exploitability, blast radius, user impact, confidence, or proven containment. The merge/block decision remains the severity contract above: `P0\n\n---\n\n## 3. EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Every `P0` and `P1` finding must include a concrete `file:line` citation.\n- Evidence must tie the finding to observed code behavior, not just a general concern.\n- `P2` findings should still include specific evidence when available, even if impact is advisory.\n- If evidence is incomplete, state the assumption and why the risk still matters.\n\n---\n\n## 4. FINDINGS OUTPUT ORDERING\n\n- Present findings before summary or praise sections.\n- Order findings by severity first: `P0`, then `P1`, then `P2`.\n- Keep ordering stable within a severity bucket by impact and confidence.\n- Separate required fixes from optional suggestions so merge decisions stay clear.\n\n---","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"9c4bda15-e128-4741-91cb-5c36ee44e695","timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:33:10.001Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\ntitle: Review Core Doctrine\ndescription: Shared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"findings first severity ordering\"\n  - \"review finding schema\"\n  - \"severity contract for merge decisions\"\n  - \"evidence file line citation\"\n  - \"baseline surface precedence\"\n  - \"baseline check families\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.5.10.0\n---\n\n# Review Core Doctrine\n\nShared doctrine consumed by both `@review` and `@deep-review`.\n\n**Ownership.** This file owns the severity ids and their meanings (section 2), the evidence rules (section 3) and the finding schema (section 7). The deep-review contract, [`review-mode-contract.yaml`](../../../system-deep-loop/deep-review/assets/review-mode-contract.yaml), is an external consumer: \n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nShared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- A `@review` or `@deep-review` pass that needs the severity contract, the evidence rules or the finding schema.\n- A review that must state its `Rule sources:` line, or apply a repository or surface override to a default-lens rule.\n\nEvery rule in sections 2 to 7 is review floor, the part no repository or surface can change. Section 8 names the floor ids, the default lens that a repository may drop or replace, and how the review reads and reports those overrides.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SEVERITY DEFINITIONS\n\n| Level | Meaning | Handling |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P0 | Blocker: exploitable security issue, auth bypass, destructive data loss | Block merge |\n| P1 | Required: correctness bug, spec mismatch, must-fix gate issue | Fix before merge |\n| P2 | Suggestion: non-blocking improvement, documentation polish, style or maintainability follow-up | Optional or schedule follow-up |\n\n### Reach Calibration\n\nA `P0` or `P1` finding names its reach: an input or call path that the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input. The stated contract is what the code promises through its signature, types, doc comment, name and the checks it already makes.\n\n- A failure that needs input outside the stated contract, such as `NaN` where the contract says a number, a wrong type, or an inverted range the code already rejects, is `P2` hardening.\n- The exception is input the code claims to validate. When a guard, a thrown error or a doc line promises to reject an input class and the promise fails for an input in that class, the gap is a correctness finding and keeps `P1` or higher. A guard claims only the class it checks: an ordering check s\n- At a trust boundary (user input, network, files, environment, another service), every value the outside can send is in reach. The stated contract of internal code never narrows a security finding on data that crosses that boundary.\n- A finding that cannot name its reach is `P2`, however severe the failure would be.\n\nEscalation rule: if confidence is low but impact is high, classify toward the higher severity and state the uncertainty explicitly. The confidence is confidence that the reach exists. The rule raises a finding whose reach is named but not proven, such as a caller that probably sends the input. It ne\n\n### Numeric Severity Calibration\n\nNumeric scores are advisory context, not the gate. A reviewer may add an optional `riskScore` to a finding and adjust it by `+/-2` for local context such as exploitability, blast radius, user impact, confidence, or proven containment. The merge/block decision remains the severity contract above: `P0\n\n---\n\n## 3. EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Every `P0` and `P1` finding must include a concrete `file:line` citation.\n- Evidence must tie the finding to observed code behavior, not just a general concern.\n- `P2` findings should still include specific evidence when available, even if impact is advisory.\n- If evidence is incomplete, state the assumption and why the risk still matters.\n\n---\n\n## 4. FINDINGS OUTPUT ORDERING\n\n- Present findings before summary or praise sections.\n- Order findings by severity first: `P0`, then `P1`, then `P2`.\n- Keep ordering stable within a severity bucket by impact and confidence.\n- Separate required fixes from optional suggestions so merge decisions stay clear.\n\n---","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V1Xg3Avc1sxdkMB1Fb1sWs","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
