{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016JUKexnccRBuMzjjHuj5nT","type":"tool_result","content":"diff --git a/.utcp_config.json b/.utcp_config.json\nindex 2df19e5c217..a10c3beceb5 100644\n--- a/.utcp_config.json\n+++ b/.utcp_config.json\n@@ -28,6 +28,22 @@\n                 }\n             }\n         },\n+        {\n+            \"name\": \"bladebro\",\n+            \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n+            \"config\": {\n+                \"mcpServers\": {\n+                    \"bladebro\": {\n+                        \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n+                        \"command\": \"bladebro\",\n+                        \"args\": [\n+                            \"mcp\"\n+                        ],\n+                        \"env\": {}\n+                    }\n+                }\n+            }\n+        },\n         {\n             \"name\": \"chrome_devtools_1\",\n             \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n### Purpose\n\nStates the manual, the trigger for using it and the discovery the agent must run first.\n\n### When to Use\n\nLoad this file when the lane rule chose MCP: the binary is unavailable, or the task needs composition with other Code Mode tools, parallel isolated sessions or an MCP-only capability.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nThe MCP lane changes the transport, not the capability. Both lanes call the same handlers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE MANUAL\n\n`.utcp_config.json` carries one manual named `bladebro`, in the same shape as the `aside` manual:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"bladebro\",\n  \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n  \"config\": {\n    \"mcpServers\": {\n      \"bladebro\": {\n        \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n        \"command\": \"bladebro\",\n        \"args\": [\"mcp\"],\n        \"env\": {}\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nConfirm it is registered rather than adding a second one:\n\n```bash\njq -c '.manual_call_templates[] | select(.name==\"bladebro\")' .utcp_config.json\n```\n\nThe manual launches the installed binary. Without a global install, an MCP client can launch it through npm with `\"command\": \"npx\"` and `\"args\": [\"-y\", \"bladebro\", \"mcp\"]`, but that pulls the package at launch, which is an install under R-SEC-3 and needs an operator yes. Set `BLADE_HOME` or `BLADE_FRESH` in `env` when a task needs isolation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DISCOVERY BEFORE INVOCATION\n\nNo callable name for this manual has been observed on this host. Do not assume one.\n\n1. Start a Code Mode session that loaded the `bladebro` manual.\n2. Run `list_tools()` or `search_tools(\"bladebro\")`, then `tool_info()` on the match.\n3. Use the `Access as:` form that `tool_info()` prints inside `call_tool_chain`.\n4. Wrap browser work in `try/finally` so the stdio process closes.\n\nThe mcp-code-mode naming convention predicts a `bladebro.bladebro_<tool>` callable. That is a prediction to confirm, not a contract.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PARALLEL ISOLATED SESSIONS\n\nEach MCP server process owns one Bladebro data root. For parallel isolated sessions, give each process its own `BLADE_HOME` or `BLADE_FRESH=1`. A second manual for that purpose is a configuration change the operator approves; it is not registered here.\n\n---\n\n## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [`cli-reference.md`](./cli-reference.md): the actions behind each tool.\n- [`install-and-verify.md`](./install-and-verify.md): the MCP lane probe.\n- `mcp-code-mode` skill: Code Mode discovery and `call_tool_chain`.\n- Upstream `docs/setup.md` (MCP section) at `<url>\n1:# 2026-11-21--manual-testing-playbook--macos-live\n3:> mcp-bladebro · manual-testing-playbook · 14-step macOS plan · Bladebro v4.8.2 on macOS arm64\n23:| BB-003 native-binary-digest | 4 | PASS | installed digest equals the v4.8.2 sidecar, `7324e502...d8fa8` |\n24:| BB-004 version-and-doctor | 5 | PASS | `bladebro v4.8.2`, doctor `12/14 PASSED · 2 ADVISORIES` |\n25:| BB-005 mcp-tools-and-code-mode | 6 | PASS | five tools over stdio, 5 tools in Code Mode, live `bladebro_see` call returned the page |\n40:The operator approved the install of `bladebro@4.8.2` pinned, with the rollback `npm uninstall -g bladebro` plus removing its data root. The answer is recorded in the packet `goal.md` before any step 3 output. Each command ran once from the repository root on macOS arm64 against `<url> Output went to a scratch folder outside the repository.\n42:The real-browser steps ran once after the operator lifted the agent-profile limit for one probe: the Aside browser binary, Aside's signed-in `Default` profile copied with `Login Data*`, `Web Data*` and `Affiliation Database*` left out so that only cookies and site storage remained, `clone` mode and example.com only. `profile` mode was never used.\n44:The scratch files hold output and exit status only, not the command lines. The `$` lines below come from the plan, the operator's account of the run and the installed `bladebro help`. Treat them as reconstructed. The output under each one is quoted from the files.\n75:$ npm install -g bladebro@4.8.2\n82:Run before any Bladebro command. The second line is the v4.8.2 release sidecar.\n93:bladebro v4.8.2 (bfdb5c3)\n96:  update available: v4.8.2 → v4.8.3\n102:BLADEBRO v4.8.2 (bfdb5c3) — System Dossier [macOS (ARM64) · npm]\n106:  ✓ Binary integrity   v4.8.2 (7.1 MB) [npm]\n120:  ⚠ Version            v4.8.2 (update available: v4.8.3)\n127:The two advisories are the profile folder that is created on first run and the 4.8.3 update. Neither is a failed check. The version is 4.8.2, so the packet flags stand, with the one correction in BB-007.\n213:{ \"attach_live\": false, \"attach_port\": 9222, \"binary\": null, \"browser\": null,\n240:The profile was first pointed at Aside's live `Default` folder, then repointed to the cookie-only staged copy before the lane turned on. No import happened while it pointed at the live folder. The status labels the browser `Google Chrome (Chrome)` even with the Aside binary override.\n332:SKIP. Blocker: removing the package and the data root `~/.local/state/blade` waits for operator approval. `bladebro` is still installed.\n339:2. `act click` accepts its target both positionally and through `--text` in v4.8.2. The packet's `--text` examples are correct.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"161d6c19-da13-478a-bb65-cafcd32eefaf","timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:35:38.727Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/.utcp_config.json b/.utcp_config.json\nindex 2df19e5c217..a10c3beceb5 100644\n--- a/.utcp_config.json\n+++ b/.utcp_config.json\n@@ -28,6 +28,22 @@\n                 }\n             }\n         },\n+        {\n+            \"name\": \"bladebro\",\n+            \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n+            \"config\": {\n+                \"mcpServers\": {\n+                    \"bladebro\": {\n+                        \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n+                        \"command\": \"bladebro\",\n+                        \"args\": [\n+                            \"mcp\"\n+                        ],\n+                        \"env\": {}\n+                    }\n+                }\n+            }\n+        },\n         {\n             \"name\": \"chrome_devtools_1\",\n             \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n### Purpose\n\nStates the manual, the trigger for using it and the discovery the agent must run first.\n\n### When to Use\n\nLoad this file when the lane rule chose MCP: the binary is unavailable, or the task needs composition with other Code Mode tools, parallel isolated sessions or an MCP-only capability.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nThe MCP lane changes the transport, not the capability. Both lanes call the same handlers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE MANUAL\n\n`.utcp_config.json` carries one manual named `bladebro`, in the same shape as the `aside` manual:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"bladebro\",\n  \"call_template_type\": \"mcp\",\n  \"config\": {\n    \"mcpServers\": {\n      \"bladebro\": {\n        \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n        \"command\": \"bladebro\",\n        \"args\": [\"mcp\"],\n        \"env\": {}\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nConfirm it is registered rather than adding a second one:\n\n```bash\njq -c '.manual_call_templates[] | select(.name==\"bladebro\")' .utcp_config.json\n```\n\nThe manual launches the installed binary. Without a global install, an MCP client can launch it through npm with `\"command\": \"npx\"` and `\"args\": [\"-y\", \"bladebro\", \"mcp\"]`, but that pulls the package at launch, which is an install under R-SEC-3 and needs an operator yes. Set `BLADE_HOME` or `BLADE_FRESH` in `env` when a task needs isolation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DISCOVERY BEFORE INVOCATION\n\nNo callable name for this manual has been observed on this host. Do not assume one.\n\n1. Start a Code Mode session that loaded the `bladebro` manual.\n2. Run `list_tools()` or `search_tools(\"bladebro\")`, then `tool_info()` on the match.\n3. Use the `Access as:` form that `tool_info()` prints inside `call_tool_chain`.\n4. Wrap browser work in `try/finally` so the stdio process closes.\n\nThe mcp-code-mode naming convention predicts a `bladebro.bladebro_<tool>` callable. That is a prediction to confirm, not a contract.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PARALLEL ISOLATED SESSIONS\n\nEach MCP server process owns one Bladebro data root. For parallel isolated sessions, give each process its own `BLADE_HOME` or `BLADE_FRESH=1`. A second manual for that purpose is a configuration change the operator approves; it is not registered here.\n\n---\n\n## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [`cli-reference.md`](./cli-reference.md): the actions behind each tool.\n- [`install-and-verify.md`](./install-and-verify.md): the MCP lane probe.\n- `mcp-code-mode` skill: Code Mode discovery and `call_tool_chain`.\n- Upstream `docs/setup.md` (MCP section) at `<url>\n1:# 2026-11-21--manual-testing-playbook--macos-live\n3:> mcp-bladebro · manual-testing-playbook · 14-step macOS plan · Bladebro v4.8.2 on macOS arm64\n23:| BB-003 native-binary-digest | 4 | PASS | installed digest e